### Introduction

`name: value` at a call site is an alternate spelling of `name := value` (examples/named_arguments.code) -- both bind an argument by the callee's declared param *name*, order-independent.
###
send_message ( person, text;
	"To `person`: `text`"
)

@assert send_message(person: 'Jack', text: 'Found fresh water') == 'To Jack: Found fresh water', 'a colon argument binds by the param''s declared name'
@assert send_message('Sayid', 'Meet at the caves') == 'To Sayid: Meet at the caves', 'naming is opt-in -- a bare positional call still works'


### `:` and `:=` can mix in one call

Both spellings bind the same way, so a call can freely use either for any argument.
###
@assert send_message(person: 'Hurley', text := ':)') == 'To Hurley: :)', 'the `:` and `:=` spellings are interchangeable'


### Positional first, then named -- never the other way

A call can start with bare positional arguments and switch to `:`/`:=` partway through, but not switch back.
###
@assert send_message('Kate', text: 'Watch the fire') == 'To Kate: Watch the fire', 'positional then named is fine'

# send_message(person: 'Kate', 'Watch the fire')  -- raises Code::Positional_Argument_After_Named


### A name that matches no declared parameter raises

###
# send_message(from: 'Jack', text: 'oops')  -- raises Code::Unknown_Named_Argument: from is not a declared parameter
